data.content:
This is a known .NET 10 breaking change. Android deprecated `adjustResize` long ago, and .NET 10 MAUI (with edge-to-edge support) no longer honors `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize`.

Here are your options, from simplest to most flexible:

---

### Option 1: Use `KeyboardAutoManagerScroll` (easiest)

MAUI 8+ auto-scrolls focused inputs above the keyboard by default. Make sure you haven't disabled it:

```xml
<!-- In your page or input control -->
<Entry
    KeyboardAutoManagerScroll.Disconnect="False"
    Placeholder="Type a message..." />
```

If your layout uses an `AbsoluteLayout` or similar, the auto-manager may not find a parent `ScrollView` to adjust. Wrap your chat content in one:

```xml
<Grid RowDefinitions="*,Auto">
    <ScrollView Grid.Row="0">
        <CollectionView ItemsSource="{Binding Messages}" ... />
    </ScrollView>

    <HorizontalStackLayout Grid.Row="1" Padding="8">
        <Entry Placeholder="Type a message..." HorizontalOptions="FillAndExpand" />
        <Button Text="Send" />
    </HorizontalStackLayout>
</Grid>
```

---

### Option 2: Handle Android insets manually (most control)

In your `Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs`:

```csharp
using Android.Views;
using AndroidX.Core.View;

public class MainActivity : MauiAppCompatActivity
{
    protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
    {
        base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);

        ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(Window!.DecorView, new InsetsListener());
    }

    private class InsetsListener : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
    {
        public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
        {
            var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
            var navInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.NavigationBars());

            // Pad the bottom by whichever is larger: keyboard or nav bar
            int bottom = Math.Max(imeInsets.Bottom, navInsets.Bottom);
            v.SetPadding(0, 0, 0, bottom);

            return insets;
        }
    }
}
```

This replicates the old `adjustResize` behavior using modern `WindowInsets` APIs — the entire content area shrinks when the keyboard opens, pushing your input bar up.

---

### Option 3: Set `adjustPan` as a fallback

In `Platforms/Android/AndroidManifest.xml`, set pan mode on your activity:

```xml
<activity
    android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustPan"
    ... />
```

This scrolls the whole screen up so the focused field is visible. It works but can look jarring in a chat UI since the toolbar scrolls off-screen.

---

**Recommendation:** For a chat screen, **Option 2** gives the best UX — the input bar rises smoothly and your chat list shrinks, just like native messaging apps.
